<TopicPage slug="subjects/sociology/advanced/globalisation-and-society">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This chapter stretches the tier's frame from societies to the planet. It assumes the stratification tools of
    the intermediate <em>Class &amp; Stratification</em> — the elephant curve is stratification drawn at world
    scale — and the identity work of <em>Race, Ethnicity &amp; Identity</em> and <em>Gender &amp; Society</em>.
    The foundational <em>Society and Socialisation</em> supplies the deepest premise: reference groups, here
    moving from the street to the planet.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>narrate globalisation's waves and explain why irreversible-arrow stories fail;</li>
    <li>define time-space compression, disembedding and the risk society, and say what each does to identity;</li>
    <li>argue glocalisation against the homogenisation thesis using the lesson's cases;</li>
    <li>read the elephant curve and the China-shock research, and connect concentrated losses to the populist backlash;</li>
    <li>state Rodrik's trilemma and Polanyi's double movement, and use each to explain a current standoff.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The intermediate tiers treated stratification within societies; this chapter stretches the frame
    to the planet. Globalisation — the stretching of economic, cultural and political relations
    across borders until distance stops being the organising fact — is not a policy but a
    transformation of social structure, and sociology asks the questions economics brackets: what
    does it do to identity, belonging, status and inequality <em>within and between</em> societies?
    The honest findings: the process is older than the buzzword, its cultural effects are
    hybridising rather than homogenising, and its distributional arithmetic — aggregate gains,
    concentrated losses — manufactured the politics now contesting it.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="waves, compression, glocalisation, backlash" widget="equationStep" config={globCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the cards run the lesson's spine — waves that rise and retreat, compression
    that moves your reference group from the street to the planet, glocalisation against the McWorld caricature,
    and the winners-losers arithmetic that manufactured the backlash. Watch card two against card four: flows
    first, then who bears them — from process to distribution is where sociology does its distinct work.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> globalisation is a transformation of social structure, not a policy. First, history:
    the process is older, and less one-way, than the buzzword.
  </p>

  <h2>Waves: globalisation is not one-way</h2>
  <p>
    The 1870–1914 wave matched today's for integration by most metrics: the gold standard fixed
    currencies, steam and telegraph collapsed transport and communication costs, capital flowed at
    ratios Britain has not seen since, and tens of millions migrated (the age of the open border —
    emigration rates from Italy and Ireland exceeded anything in the modern era). It ended in
    1914 — war, then tariffs, depression and the collapse of liberal order — establishing the
    wave-like pattern: integration, crisis, retreat, redesign. The post-1945 wave was deliberately
    <em>managed</em> (Bretton Woods: capital controls tolerated, trade liberalised, welfare states
    cushioning) — Rodrik calls it the compromise of embedded liberalism. The current wave from the
    1980s is the hyperglobalised variant: capital mobility, container shipping and the internet,
    with China's 2001 WTO accession as its emblem. And the 2010s–2020s brought measurable deceleration
    — trade peaked as a share of world GDP around 2008, finance re-shored, "slowbalisation" and
    friend-shoring became policy vocabulary. Anyone narrating globalisation as an irreversible arrow
    has not read its history.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "globalisation is a modern invention, an irreversible arrow that started in
  the 1990s." It seems reasonable — the current wave's vocabulary (containers, the internet, the WTO) is
  recent. But the 1870–1914 wave matched today's integration by most metrics and collapsed in 1914; the
  post-1945 wave was deliberately managed; and the 2010s–2020s brought measurable deceleration, with trade
  peaking as a share of world GDP around 2008. The correct picture: integration moves in waves — rise, crisis,
  retreat, redesign — so irreversibility arguments start from a history the record does not support.</p>

  <h2>Time-space compression and the self under stretch</h2>
  <p>
    Harvey's <strong>time-space compression</strong> names the core sociological transformation: as
    transport, finance and communication accelerate, distance stops structuring social life the way
    it did. Giddens adds <strong>disembedding</strong> — relations lifted out of local contexts and
    reorganized across time-space (money, expertise systems, media), so that a teenager's reference
    group is global video, not the street. Beck's <strong>risk society</strong> extends it: the
    defining risks (climate, financial contagion, pandemics) are manufactured by modernity and
    distributed by interdependence, not geography — Chernobyl and 2008 and 2020 did not respect
    borders, and the institutional problem is that risk is planetary while governance is national.
    For identity the effects are double: <strong>hybridisation</strong> (diasporic, dual, code-
    switching selves — Appadurai's five scapes of global flow: ethnoscapes, mediascapes, technoscapes,
    financescapes, ideoscapes) alongside reactive <strong>re-embedding</strong> — the local
    sharpening itself against the global (Brexit as identity statement as much as trade policy).
    Neither effect is secondary; both are what globalisation does to the social self.
  </p>

  <h2>Culture: glocalisation beats McWorld</h2>
  <p>
    The homogenisation thesis (one world, one consumer culture) predicted poorly. What the evidence
    shows is <strong>glocalisation</strong> (Robertson): global forms — fast food, formats, genres —
    arrive and are locally reworked, while local forms globalise on their own terms. McDonald's
    menus index national taste from Mumbai (McAloo Tikki) to Tokyo; hip-hop left the Bronx and
    returned as a hundred local musics; Korean popular culture built a state-assisted export
    industry that now sets templates for the West; Nollywood became the world's second-largest film
    industry by volume with distribution networks of its own. Appadurai's deeper point holds: the
    five scapes flow at different speeds and in different directions, and their disjunctures — media
    images outrunning migration possibilities, capital fleeing faster than labour can follow —
    generate the frictions local studies actually document. Cultural imperialism theory survives in
    modified form (platform ownership, language dominance, Hollywood's box-office gravity are real
    structural advantages) but the one-way street is dead.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the flows are real but hybridising — global forms locally reworked, local forms
    globalised. Next: who gained, who stagnated, and what that does to politics.
  </p>

  <h2>Inequality between and within societies</h2>
  <p>
    The distributional record of the current wave is the syllabus's sharpest question. Globally,
    the era was a triumph: extreme poverty fell from over a third of humanity in 1990 to under a
    tenth pre-pandemic, driven overwhelmingly by Asian growth — the largest poverty reduction in
    recorded history. Between-country inequality narrowed for the first time in two centuries
    (convergence is the new fact of the world income distribution). But the <strong>elephant
    curve</strong> (Lakner and Milanovic) shows the winners were the emerging-world middle (the
    elephant's rising back) and the global top 1% (the trunk), while the 80th–85th percentiles —
    predominantly the lower-middle of rich countries — stagnated (the valley). Add Autor's
    <strong>China-shock</strong> research: import competition destroyed US manufacturing employment
    in exposed regions with weak, slow adjustment — localised, durable, and not compensated by
    diffuse national gains. Sociology's translation: the losers were concentrated enough to
    <em>become</em> a political subject — status threat plus material decline powering the populist
    wave (Rodrik's trilemma explains the structural bind: deep integration, national sovereignty and
    democratic politics — pick two; the euro crisis, Brexit and China's model each resolve the
    trilemma differently).
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "globalisation = free trade." It is a bundle — trade, capital
    mobility, migration regimes, intellectual property, institutional harmonisation — and the
    components distribute differently. The 1945–1980 wave liberalised trade while taming capital;
    the post-1980 wave did the reverse. Observed outcomes (rising within-country inequality in most
    OECD states, with the US and UK pulling far ahead of Germany and Japan) depend on which bundle
    a country ran and what its domestic institutions did with the gains — the institutional
    difference is the variable, not globalisation per se.
  </p>

  <h2>What has been tried against the inequalities</h2>
  <p>
    The syllabus asks what attempts have been made, and the honest ledger spans scales. Globally:
    the SDGs and aid architecture (with the recurring evidence that aid is marginal to growth);
    debt-relief regimes (HIPC) that measurably freed fiscal space; preferential trade access for
    the poorest; and the hard-won pharmaceutical flexibilities (TRIPS and the 2001 Doha declaration
    on public health) that restructured IP for poor countries. Regionally, the EU's structural
    funds are the largest deliberate redistribution programme between states anywhere, with
    measurable convergence effects where institutions absorb them well. Nationally, the observed
    distributional difference between social models (Nordic versus Anglo-liberal) shows
    domestic policy still dominates national income distributions — globalisation constrains
    tax competition at the top, but the within-country spread remains a policy choice, not a
    fate. Migration is the missing instrument: wage gaps dwarf trade gains, and labour mobility
    would equalise more than any other mechanism — which is precisely why it is the most politically
    restricted flow of the era (the capital-free, labour-managed asymmetry is globalisation's
    defining hypocrisy and its central sociological fact).
  </p>

  <h2>Migration: the most governed, least free flow</h2>
  <p>
    Migration is globalisation's demographic face and its sharpest asymmetry: capital and
    goods move under liberalised rules, people under the world's most restrictive regimes.
    The numbers: roughly 281 million international migrants (3.6% of humanity, remarkably
    stable as a share across decades), with remittances to low- and middle-income countries
    (~$650 billion a year) exceeding foreign direct investment and development aid combined —
    migration is the largest redistributive flow in the world economy. The sociology:
    transnationalism as lived structure (dual households, remittance obligations, circular
    return), diaspora institutions, and the segmented labour markets migrants enter (the dual
    labour market finding — migrants concentrate in jobs natives vacate as they exit upward).
    The politics: the gap between economic evidence (migration raises aggregate output; wage
    effects on natives are small and concentrated among prior migrants — the Mariel boatlift
    literature's contested natural experiment) and political perception (magnitudes far
    larger) is the era's central democratic dilemma. The policy instruments (points systems,
    asylum regimes, border externalisation to transit states) show states retaining real
    control — at rising moral and fiscal cost — and climate displacement (scenario ranges
    reaching hundreds of millions by mid-century, predominantly internal) will test whether
    the managed-migration machinery scales at all.
  </p>

  <h2>Global governance and its deficits</h2>
  <p>
    Interdependence outruns its institutions, and the gap has names. <strong>Tax</strong>:
    profit shifting to havens costs governments hundreds of billions annually; the OECD's 15%
    global minimum corporate tax (agreed 2021, implementing unevenly) is the first systemic
    response in a century. <strong>Financial</strong>: the 2008 crisis produced Basel III's
    capital rules — the lesson that regulation follows crisis, not foresight.
    <strong>Trade</strong>: the WTO's dispute system paralysed (the Appellate Body story from
    the political-economy lesson) while regional blocs deepened. <strong>Health</strong>:
    pandemic governance (the WHO's limited enforcement, the vaccine nationalism of 2021)
    documented the sovereignty cost of preparedness. <strong>Climate</strong>: Paris's
    pledge-and-review architecture trades binding targets for universal participation — the
    design bets that transparency and ratcheting substitute for enforcement, and the ICJ's
    climate-obligations advisory opinion now gives that bet a legal spine. The sociological
    reading: governance deficits are distributional decisions — who bears the externality is
    chosen by default — and the populist backlash is, in part, a demand that the defaults be
    decided democratically.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "globalisation means states are powerless — the market decides everything
  now." It seems reasonable — capital, goods and data cross borders voters cannot. But the lesson's
  record shows states and interstate bodies constantly redesigning the rules — the OECD global minimum tax,
  Basel III, the Paris architecture, border externalisation — and retaining real control where they choose to,
  migration above all. The correct picture: governance is lagging and uneven, not absent; its deficits are
  distributional decisions — who bears the externality is chosen by default — which is why the backlash
  demands the defaults be decided democratically.</p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Name the three corners of Rodrik's trilemma, and explain how the euro crisis and China's model resolve
    it differently.</li>
    <li>The elephant curve shows huge gains for the emerging-world middle and the top 1%, with stagnation around
    the 80th–85th percentiles. Why did that shape produce backlash politics rather than quiet satisfaction with
    the aggregate?</li>
    <li>Harvey, Giddens and Beck each name a transformation — compression, disembedding, risk society. Pick one
    and explain what it changes about the self.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Deep economic integration, national sovereignty and democratic politics —
    pick two. The euro area chose integration over national monetary sovereignty; China keeps
    sovereignty and integration while suspending democratic politics; each abandons a different corner. (2) Because the gains were concentrated and so were the losses: Autor's China-shock work
    shows import competition destroying manufacturing employment in exposed regions, weakly compensated by
    diffuse national gains. Concentrated losers become a political subject: status threat plus material decline
    is the populist wave's fuel. (3) Compression: as transport and communication accelerate, distance stops
    structuring life, so your reference group is no longer your street — comparison runs globally, and
    identity responds by hybridising and by re-embedding the local against the global.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Globalisation moves in waves — 1870–1914, managed post-1945, hyperglobalised post-1980, decelerating since the 2010s — nothing about it is one-way.</li>
    <li>Compression and disembedding move your reference group from street to planet; the risk society makes risk planetary while governance stays national.</li>
    <li>Culture glocalises: global forms are locally reworked and local forms globalise — the one-way street of cultural imperialism is dead.</li>
    <li>The elephant curve: aggregate triumph, concentrated pain — the China shock made losers local, durable and political.</li>
    <li>Rodrik's trilemma: deep integration, sovereignty, democracy — pick two; Polanyi's double movement makes the backlash endogenous.</li>
    <li>Migration is the largest redistributive flow (remittances exceed FDI and aid combined) and the most restricted one.</li>
    <li>Governance deficits are distributional decisions — who bears the externality is chosen by default.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    <em>Social Networks</em> is the sibling advanced lesson: networks are the micro-machinery behind these flows
    — how ties carry migration, diffusion and backlash alike. The expert tier's social
    theory and demography lessons pick up the two traditions this chapter set against each other, Polanyi and
    world-systems; and the intermediate <em>Class &amp; Stratification</em> rereads as the within-society half of
    the elephant curve.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Theoretical scaffolding.</strong> Polanyi's <strong>double movement</strong> (The Great
    Transformation, 1944) is the frame that keeps explaining: markets disembedded from social
    relations generate insecurity, society protects itself (welfare states, then regulation, then
    backlash); the late-19th-century wave, the 1930s retreat and today's populism are the same
    dialectic recurring — globalisation produces its own critics endogenously. Wallerstein's
    <strong>world-systems theory</strong> (core, semi-periphery, periphery; unequal exchange)
    explains persistent hierarchy where neoclassical convergence predicts smoothing — the two
    traditions still divide the field. Sassen's <strong>global cities</strong> reframed geography:
    command functions (finance, law, advanced services) concentrate in a few city-regions whose
    interests diverge from their own national interiors — London's City versus the North, the
    coastal metros versus the interior — giving the backlash its electoral geography.
    <strong>Measurement disputes.</strong> Whether global inequality is truly falling depends on
    weighting (population-weighted convergence falls; unweighted country inequality has not),
    and household-survey top incomes understate the very top — adjust for tax data and the
    elephant's trunk grows. <strong>Open questions:</strong> does deglobalisation mean retreat or
    regional reorganisation (regional trade shares keep rising); can welfare states survive tax
    competition and mobile capital without global coordination (the OECD minimum-tax deal is the
    live experiment); how climate migration — hundreds of millions in scenario ranges by 2050 —
    will interact with the era's already-taut migration politics; and whether the platform economy
    constitutes a new wave (data flows unmeasured by trade statistics now carry the integration)
    — the next decade's measurement problem, already under construction.
  </p>
  <p>
    The cultural-political economy of platforms completes the picture, because the current
    wave's infrastructure is digital. Data flows carry integration that trade statistics miss
    (cross-border data traffic grew by orders of magnitude while goods trade plateaued — the
    digital-globalisation estimates), and the platform layer reorganises the older flows:
    labour platforms move work rather than workers across borders, content platforms globalise
    culture with recommendation systems as the new gatekeepers, and financial platforms
    (fintech, stablecoins) experiment with capital mobility beyond the regulatory perimeter.
    The sociology of this layer is being written now: attention as the scarce resource,
    moderation as the new trade policy (what may circulate), and the geopolitical bifurcation
    of platform ecosystems — the GDPR sphere, the US commercial sphere, the
    sovereign-internet sphere — as the new blocs. Globalisation's next chapter will be
    measured as much in data governance as in tariffs.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="check yourself: the elephant curve, the trilemma, glocalisation, Polanyi" widget="quiz" config={globQuiz} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the four items are the lesson's load-bearing evidence and frames — who the
    elephant curve's flat stretch belongs to, what Rodrik's trilemma trades off, what the McAloo Tikki example
    illustrates, and what Polanyi's double movement predicts. If any answer felt like a guess, reread the
    matching section — each wrong option is a real position the evidence was marshalled against.
  </p>

